WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning actor

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.137
you know from
"American Horror Story"

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:05.905
and "American Crime Story"
franchises.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.474 line:0%
She's currently
starring in "Appropriate,"

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.609 line:0%
which is playing on Broadway now

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.511 line:0%
at Second Stage's Hayes Theater.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.945
Please welcome back to the show,
our very good friend

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:14.714
Sarah Paulson, everybody!

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.783
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:36.035 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:39.204
Welcome back, my dear friend.
-Thank you. How are you?

00:00:39.204 --> 00:00:41.274
-I'm so happy to have you
in New York City doing a show.

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.042
-Thank you. Yeah.

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.945
-But also,
I'm very excited to hear that

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.380
you're staying
in my neighborhood.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.682
We're like, basically neighbors.
-I am. Basically neighbors.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.483
And I walk my dogs
400 times a day,

00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:53.219
and I've never seen you.
-You haven't seen me once.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.255
-So it just begs the question,
where is your dog urinating?

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.924
-Yeah.
-Because I know you have a dog.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:59.458
-You know I have a dog.
You love my dog.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:00.527
You made me love --

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.895
It's what's called
a reverse Samberg,

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.163
how much you love my dog.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:05.698
-I really love your dog.
-You really love my dog.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.534
My dog is a pad-trained.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:09.836
I know.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.270
-Disgusting.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.506
-Yeah.
-I'm just kidding.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.575
I mean, whatever you need to do.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:16.943
But also, you know my dog --

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.645
my dog doesn't want to be
outside in this weather.

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.947
-Yeah, your dog is like,
a little, skinny mini.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:23.215
-My dog has to wear
a sweater outside.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:24.750
Basically, the only time
she does is like,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.052
June 15th to August 15th.

00:01:27.052 --> 00:01:28.754
It's like, two months where
she's allowed to be outside.

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:29.889
I'm very impressed,

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.057
and we were talking
about it backstage,

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.858
and I know you obviously
had no choice

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:33.927
based on how much
you love your dogs.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.127
-Yes.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:36.262
-You brought all three of
them out from California.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:37.296
-I did.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:39.299
I have three rescue dogs,

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.568
and they are the absolute
loves of my life.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.869
[ Applause ]
-Great.

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:46.673
-Thank you.
But they're California dogs.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:49.875
So like, every time
I take them outside here,

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:51.677
it is quite an experience.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:55.314
It takes me like, 30 minutes
to put their little sweater on,

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:56.849
and they just really
look at me like,

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:58.117
"Why are we doing this?"

00:01:58.117 --> 00:01:59.518
It's hell for them.

00:01:59.518 --> 00:02:00.953
-Do they travel well?

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:02.455
-I'd never flown
with them on an airplane before.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.589
-Okay.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:05.791
I actually had one
very great experience

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:07.593
with just my one dog, Louise,

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:10.629
who is the more emotional --
I would say troubled.

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:12.098
Let's say troubled.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.667
I'm her emotional support human.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.635
And I took her
out of her little bag,

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:20.406
and I showed her, like,
had her look outside the window.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.808
And it was very clear
to me the moment

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:24.611
she realized she was like,
30,000 feet off the ground,

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:25.878
She did this whole, like --

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:28.247
And I was like, that's right.

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:30.215
You're very smart because,
you know this is not normal.

00:02:30.215 --> 00:02:31.984
We're not
supposed to be up here.

00:02:31.984 --> 00:02:34.520
-By the way, you're a terrible
emotional support human.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.622
-Yeah, I'm like, "Look at it!"
-"Look at it!

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:39.425
You think it's bad in that bag?
You're in the air!"

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.028
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.897
My wife knows how much
you love Frisbee.

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.965
And she, I think maybe saw you
and said,

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.499
"Sarah Paulson's
in the neighborhood.

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:50.737
Try to give her Frisbee."

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:52.605
-I don't know
what to say about you

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:53.939
wanting to give your dog away.

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:55.474
-I don't want to give it away.
My wife does.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:57.510
-Wow.
-It's terrible.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.712
The problem is, we have three
kids and the dog...

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.713
-You have three kids now?
-Yeah.

00:03:01.713 --> 00:03:03.483
-I think
I only knew about the two.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.850
-Yeah.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.353
Now you understand why
we want to give the dog away?

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.687
-Yes, I do now.
Now it makes sense.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:09.955
-Yeah.
-I will take the dog.

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:11.390
-Gotcha.
We're just going to find you.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.326
We're gonna find you in the park
with your three dogs,

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.395
we're going to sneak
the leash into your hand,

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:17.162
and you won't even know when
you get home that you're like,

00:03:17.162 --> 00:03:18.698
"Do I have four now?"

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.434
This is fantastic
to have you back on Broadway.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.602
-Thank you.

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:26.405
Broadway audiences, lovely,
enthusiastic.

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:28.274
-Sure. Yes. But also...

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.608
-Okay, great.
-I'm sorry.

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:33.679
I have to just take the wheel.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:37.249
The people in the audience
are very loud.

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:38.951
-Yeah.
-I think you think

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.786
as an audience member,
and maybe --

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:43.689
I mean, I don't think this,
but it seems like they do --

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.257
that there's like,
a glass partition

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:47.259
between the audience
and the stage.

00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:48.994
-That you can't hear them.

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:50.062
-That I can't hear you.
-Yeah.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:52.965
-I hear
every sound you're making.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:57.637
I hear your sighing.
I hear your frustration.

00:03:57.637 --> 00:03:59.305
There are phones
going off all the time.

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:00.405
And then there's the lady
who's like,

00:04:00.405 --> 00:04:02.007
"Turn off your phone."

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:03.676
And I'm like, what?

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:06.913
You have just made a bad
situation exponentially worse

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.148
by shouting at full volume.

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:10.316
Also, there's the, like,

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:11.851
the cough,
the freedom of the cough,

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:14.887
which is like, once one person
finally lets out their cough

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:17.089
they've been stifling
for two hours,

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:20.392
then 16 people are like --
[ Coughing ]

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:23.129
All of a sudden in the
quietest moment of the play,

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.331
it's just like,
a cacophony of coughs.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:26.365
It's hell on earth.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.367
I just feel like I have to ask

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:33.539
for a little bit of awareness
of, we are up there,

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.375
doing the play for you,
whilst thinking about like,

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:38.277
what we might have for dinner
or when the show is over.

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:40.246
There's a lot going on
in the human brain

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:41.948
while you're doing the play.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.316
It doesn't help
if I see the person

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:44.316
in the third row doing this.

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:46.853
I can see you. I see you.

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.755
I can see you,
I can hear you, I feel you.

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:50.756
Your energy is all part
of our experience,

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:53.592
and I just feel like
I need to make a --

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:55.028
This is my public service
announcement, yeah, yeah.

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:57.063
-It's great. It's coming across.

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.765
-Is it?
-I like the observation

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:00.733
about that person
who thinks they're a hero

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:01.934
when they yell,
"Turn off your phone."

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:03.636
-Yes.
-Because I will say this.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:07.072
I don't think anybody's phone
goes off in a Broadway theater

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:08.741
on purpose,
you know what I mean?

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.276
-Yes, they're not like,

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.544
"Can't wait to do this
in Act Three." Bing.

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:13.646
-No.

00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:15.214
-Someone's like,
"Turn off your phone!"

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:16.848
They're like,
"I'm expecting a call!"

00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:19.852
No one yells that back.
-No, they don't, they don't.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:24.190
But this has been a very --
The play is quite, um, raucous.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.058
There are many things
that happen that cause

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.895
audience responses that are kind
of amazing for us as actors.

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:31.096
-In a good way?
-In a good way.

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:32.899
-And then other times,
it's like --

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:36.001
The character I'm playing is
a complicated woman,

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.671
and let's just say,
sometimes there are

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.506
audience reactions
that are unpleasant for me

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.910
because they applaud
when I'm told off and it's like,

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:45.844
"Oh, I see.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:48.447
You agree with Rachel
in the play

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:51.884
that I may have some
sort of psychological trouble."

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.218
And then I have
this sort of thing to do

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:55.354
at the end of the play
that is difficult to do

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:56.956
when I feel I have enemies
in the audience.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:59.592
-Interesting.
-Yes, so...

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:02.161
-I have read that you had
a unique inspiration

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.962
for your character,
this complicated person.

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:05.164
-I did, Meredith Marks

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:06.666
from "The Real Housewives
of Salt Lake City."

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.267
-Oh, good. Great.
[ Cheering ]

00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:11.370
-It's that and "Chimp Empire,"
which was on Netflix.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:12.805
I don't know if
anybody has watched that.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:14.806
It's excellent. It's about
a bunch of chimpanzees.

00:06:14.806 --> 00:06:18.677
So if you figure out somewhere
between "Chimp Empire"

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:20.245
and "Real Housewives
of Salt Lake City,"

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:23.482
I found my character right
in the middle of that somehow.

00:06:23.482 --> 00:06:24.884
So I'm not playing
Meredith Marks,

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.853
but I was inspired
by some of her physicality

00:06:27.853 --> 00:06:29.254
and some of her intensity.

00:06:29.254 --> 00:06:31.591
-Gotcha.
-Right.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:33.426
-Now no one can make fun of me
for watching the Housewives,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:36.028
which I've been doing
since its inception, basically.

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:37.096
-From the beginning?
-From the beginning.

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:38.463
-Are you a genuine fan, like,

00:06:38.463 --> 00:06:41.166
would you be genuinely excited
if you met one?

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:44.069
-Heather Gay came to
the Sunday matinee of our show

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:45.471
and didn't come backstage,

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.640
but I saw her Instagram stories
during my intermission.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:52.744
I probably shouldn't tell people
that I'm looking at Instagram --

00:06:52.744 --> 00:06:54.247
-You're telling the audience,
like, "I'm doing a show!"

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:56.615
Meanwhile, you're backstage...
-I'm like --

00:06:56.615 --> 00:06:58.351
Like, dislike.

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:01.654
Oh, where is she?
Oh, she's in Paris. How fun.

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:03.990
No, I'm very --
I'm really focused.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.791
But Heather Gay
was in the audience,

00:07:05.791 --> 00:07:08.060
and then didn't come backstage,
because she felt too shy.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.528
Meanwhile, during intermission,

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:11.630
I went to everybody, knocking on
everyone's dressing rooms,

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.466
being like, "Heather Gay is
here. Heather Gay is here."

00:07:14.466 --> 00:07:16.169
And people were
completely flipped out,

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:17.235
and we were all excited
and figuring out

00:07:17.235 --> 00:07:18.304
what we were going to do.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.871
Lo and behold,
Steven Spielberg was also

00:07:19.871 --> 00:07:21.306
in the audience that day,

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:24.510
and people were like,
"Great. Where's Heather Gay?"

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.645
And I told Heather
this on Instagram,

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.380
and I was like,
"You better come backstage."

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.815
And she was like,
"I'm just too shy."

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:31.817
I was like, "Too shy?
I've seen like,

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:34.019
almost all of your body parts
on national television.

00:07:34.019 --> 00:07:35.621
Like, what are you
talking about?"

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.657
-It's too late for shy.
-It's too late for shy, babe.

00:07:38.657 --> 00:07:41.894
-Here's another PSA to,
let's call them

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:43.762
celebrity guests
at a Broadway show.

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:45.197
-Yes.
-You should always go backstage.

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:46.331
-You got to go.

00:07:46.331 --> 00:07:47.400
And Heather Gay
maybe doesn't feel that

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.068
she qualifies, but she does.

00:07:49.068 --> 00:07:51.670
And to me, it was like,
we got all excited,

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.607
and she didn't come back, so...

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:58.444
-Shame on her.
-Shame on you, Heather Gay.

00:07:58.444 --> 00:08:00.946
-I think she appreciates that
she made an error --

00:08:00.946 --> 00:08:02.348
-I think she's
going to rectify it.

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:05.184
-Yeah.
-Right? Yeah.

00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:07.787
-If there's one thing I know
about Real Housewives,

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:11.590
it's that they learn and change.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:13.492
That's the one takeaway
of that show.

00:08:13.492 --> 00:08:15.294
Everybody's always
self-improvement.

00:08:15.294 --> 00:08:18.397
The arrow is pointing up
for everybody.

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:20.333
-You have a wonderful cast --
Corey Stoll --

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:22.101
-Corey Stoll, Elle Fanning.

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:24.336
-Elle Fanning, I just heard,
she was on Jimmy Fallon,

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:25.738
talking about you
making her break.

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:28.407
You do a fair amount of yelling.

00:08:28.407 --> 00:08:31.010
-I do a fair amount of yelling
in this play.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.547
It is true, and so vocally,
it's challenging.

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:36.715
And so what happens
sometimes on a matinee day,

00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:40.186
when my voice is not
appropriately warmed up

00:08:40.186 --> 00:08:42.654
and I don't have
all the things...

00:08:42.654 --> 00:08:44.390
Truly, I went into
your bathroom here today,

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:45.957
and I just started going,
like, bbbbbt,

00:08:45.957 --> 00:08:47.092
like, warming up my voice

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:48.694
to get ready to do the play --
Anyway.

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:50.429
-The public one,
not my personal bathroom.

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:52.465
-No, I went into your bathroom.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.936
I just wanted to touch
all your stuff.

00:08:56.936 --> 00:09:00.839
Anyway, my point is,
I do a lot of yelling

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:03.242
and a lot of, uh,
cracky voice things happen

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:05.377
on a Saturday matinee,
a Wednesday matinee.

00:09:05.377 --> 00:09:06.879
One time I went on stage

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:09.648
and I said the line,
"Alright, River," is my line.

00:09:09.648 --> 00:09:11.317
Elle plays plays
a character named River.

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:14.486
And instead it came out --
[ Distorted ] "Alright, River."

00:09:14.486 --> 00:09:16.088
And Elle was just like...

00:09:16.088 --> 00:09:19.692
She looked at me,
and I was like, "Oh, shoot."

00:09:19.692 --> 00:09:22.895
And then she gave me
so much crap about it,

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:25.131
and we laughed
very much about it.

00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:26.899
And then
that night's performance,

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:28.067
the Saturday evening
performance,

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:30.136
she has a line
where she says, "My mother."

00:09:30.136 --> 00:09:31.737
And instead she said,
[ Distorted ] "My mother."

00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:33.572
And I was like --

00:09:33.572 --> 00:09:35.741
So these things
happen on stage sometimes.

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:37.275
The horrible part of it

00:09:37.275 --> 00:09:39.712
is that you're supposed to act
like nothing happened,

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:41.013
whereas in life, I would
have been like,

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:42.081
[ Distorted ] "Your mother."

00:09:42.081 --> 00:09:44.549
I would have done a whole thing.

00:09:44.549 --> 00:09:46.051
But you have to act
like it's not happening,

00:09:46.051 --> 00:09:47.152
which is so bizarre.

00:09:47.152 --> 00:09:49.054
-It's also
so unfair to the playwright,

00:09:49.054 --> 00:09:50.923
who had never understood --
-By the way,

00:09:50.923 --> 00:09:53.792
the playwright was there
the first time Elle laughed,

00:09:53.792 --> 00:09:55.093
because Elle did something

00:09:55.093 --> 00:09:57.396
and I went like this to her,
and she started to laugh.

00:09:57.396 --> 00:09:59.098
And we both broke up
in front of --

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:01.200
And I think our playwright
was really like,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.001
"Please don't do that again.

00:10:03.001 --> 00:10:04.903
People have paid
good, hard money

00:10:04.903 --> 00:10:06.004
to come see a Broadway play."

00:10:06.004 --> 00:10:07.506
And we're up there like --

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:08.974
We're so funny to one another,

00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:10.409
Meanwhile, the audience is like,

00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:12.878
"We don't know what you're
laughing about, first of all.

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:14.880
And second of all, that's not
the story of the play."

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.249
But maybe they sort of
feel excited a little bit,

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:19.718
like they're seeing a secret
thing that happened onstage.

00:10:19.718 --> 00:10:21.353
Right?
I don't know what this is about.

00:10:21.353 --> 00:10:22.521
-I know.
-I like to do that.

00:10:22.521 --> 00:10:23.689
-This has been an interview

00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.090
that's gone full circle
where you're like,

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:26.792
"The audience is bad."
-The audience, you're terrible!

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:29.695
I don't care about them!
-I'm like, Instagramming.

00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:32.731
I don't know what's happening.

00:10:32.731 --> 00:10:34.600
-I'm so happy to have you here.
-Thank you.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:35.867
-I'm so happy to have you
on my street.

00:10:35.867 --> 00:10:37.503
I am going to go
to the park with Frisbee.

00:10:37.503 --> 00:10:38.870
I want her to meet your dogs.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.706
-I think we've decided
your dog is now my dog.

00:10:41.706 --> 00:10:42.708
-I think we have too,

00:10:42.708 --> 00:10:45.143
which just mean the world to me.

00:10:45.143 --> 00:10:46.745
-Don't say I never
did anything for you.

00:10:46.745 --> 00:10:47.946
-Oh, I never would.
-Took your dog away from you.

00:10:47.946 --> 00:10:49.215
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:50.416
-Thanks for having me.
-It's so great to see you.

00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:53.653
You guys, Sarah Paulson.
She's the best.

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:54.720
"Appropriate" is
now playing on Broadway

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.322
at Second Stage's Hayes Theater.

00:10:57.322 --> 00:10:58.557
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Punkie Johnson.

00:10:58.557 --> 00:11:00.059
That's your face.

